UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 4: Dance elements and technique', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk. Dynamics are the quality of the action, and that quality communicates an idea. A sharp arm can suggest refusal, surprise or a stop signal. A sustained arm can suggest calm, sadness or patience. The idea comes from the quality, not from a costume or a louder count. Write the quality words if you do not perform them.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTwo dancers both raise one arm. Dancer A snaps the arm up and freezes. Dancer B lifts the arm in one smooth, even path. What idea might each dynamic communicate, and what is missing if the note only says \"they raised an arm\"?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting. Writing only \"they raised an arm\" hides the dynamic, so the audience effect cannot be checked. Name sharp or sustained and attach one clear idea to each. A written label is enough evidence.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAny fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed. A slow action can still be sharp if it jerks and stops. A quick action can be sustained if the path stays smooth. Quality is the edge and the continuity, not only the clock.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nGive one new sideways step a sharp version and a sustained version, each with its own idea. Do not reuse the head turn or the raised arm. Describe both versions if you stay seated.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Dance","learning_intention":"Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk. Dynamics are the quality of the action, and that quality communicates an idea. A sharp arm can suggest refusal, surprise or a stop signal. A sustained arm can suggest calm, sadness or patience. The idea comes from the quality, not from a costume or a louder count. Write the quality words if you do not perform them.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DA4-PER-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Give one new sideways step a sharp version and a sustained version, each with its own idea. Do not reuse the head turn or the raised arm. Describe both versions if you stay seated. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using sharp dynamic on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed.. Correction: The snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed. A slow action can still be sharp if it jerks and stops. A quick action can be sustained if the path stays smooth. Quality is the edge and the continuity, not only the clock. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating sharp dynamic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with sharp dynamic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about sharp dynamic with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for sharp dynamic. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for sharp dynamic. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting","Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"Two dancers both raise one arm. Dancer A snaps the arm up and freezes. Dancer B lifts the arm in one smooth, even path. What idea might each dynamic communicate, and what is missing if the note only says \"they raised an arm\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for sharp dynamic; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting","Careful use of sustained dynamic with clear evidence","Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named sustained dynamic, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Name sustained dynamic only, without linking it to sharp dynamic","Apply sharp dynamic to new material and name the sustained dynamic used"],"question":"Which response best applies sharp dynamic with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on sharp dynamic. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","The snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for sharp dynamic","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed","Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed","Name sustained dynamic only, with no linked example","Give one new sideways step a sharp version and a sustained version, each with its own idea","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies sharp dynamic to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about sharp dynamic with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating sharp dynamic on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using sharp dynamic on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for sharp dynamic. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with sharp dynamic, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed.. Correction: The snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-dance-y9-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Dance 7–10 (2023) outcome DA4-PER-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Movement is optional, pain-free, and adult-supervised. Written plans are acceptable evidence.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise sharp dynamic.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use sharp dynamic with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify sustained dynamic and explain why it matters.","I can practise movement quality independently on a new prompt.","I can notice dynamic choice needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over four even counts, and that sustained path can say \"I am listening\". One body part changes only its dynamic, so the audience can see where the idea came from. Jo labels the sharp version \"refusal\" and the sustained version \"patience\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nTwo dancers both raise one arm. Dancer A snaps the arm up and freezes. Dancer B lifts the arm in one smooth, even path. What idea might each dynamic communicate, and what is missing if the note only says \"they raised an arm\"?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting. Writing only \"they raised an arm\" hides the dynamic, so the audience effect cannot be checked. Name sharp or sustained and attach one clear idea to each. A written label is enough evidence.","explain":"Sharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk. Dynamics are the quality of the action, and that quality communicates an idea. A sharp arm can suggest refusal, surprise or a stop signal. A sustained arm can suggest calm, sadness or patience. The idea comes from the quality, not from a costume or a louder count. Write the quality words if you do not perform them.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed. A slow action can still be sharp if it jerks and stops. A quick action can be sustained if the path stays smooth. Quality is the edge and the continuity, not only the clock. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast equating fast with sharp and noticing that a slow jerk still has a sharp edge.","caption":"Sharp and sustained describe edges and continuity. They are not only another name for fast and slow.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Speed is quality</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Fast always means sharp</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edge and flow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">A slow jerk can be sharp</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: A sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: A sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over four even counts, and that sustained path can say \"I am listening\". One body part changes only its dynamic, so the audience can see where the idea came from. Jo labels the sharp version \"refusal\" and the sustained version \"patience\". Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two panels show a sudden stop communicating refusal and an even sustained path communicating waiting.","caption":"Both dynamics are useful. A sharp edge and a sustained flow communicate different ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sharp dynamic</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Sudden stop can say no</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sustained dynamic</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Even flow can say wait</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAny fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed. A slow action can still be sharp if it jerks and stops. A quick action can be sustained if the path stays smooth. Quality is the edge and the continuity, not only the clock. Here is a more helpful example: A sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over four even counts, and that sustained path can say \"I am listening\". One body part changes only its dynamic, so the audience can see where the idea came from. Jo labels the sharp version \"refusal\" and the sustained version \"patience\".\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe snapped arm can signal alarm, while the smooth lift can signal ongoing care or a slow greeting. Writing only \"they raised an arm\" hides the dynamic, so the audience effect cannot be checked. Name sharp or sustained and attach one clear idea to each. A written label is enough evidence.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Any fast movement is called sharp and any slow movement is called sustained, because dynamics are treated as speed. A slow action can still be sharp if it jerks and stops. A quick action can be sustained if the path stays smooth. Quality is the edge and the continuity, not only the clock. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sharp dynamic?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSharp movement has sudden edges, while sustained movement continues smoothly without a jerk. Dynamics are the quality of the action, and that quality communicates an idea. A sharp arm can suggest refusal, surprise or a stop signal. A sustained arm can suggest calm, sadness or patience. The idea comes from the quality, not from a costume or a louder count. Write the quality words if you do not perform them. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 4: Dance elements and technique","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Dance elements and technique","week":3,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA sharp head turn that stops at once can clearly say \"no\" before any words are needed. Jo then lowers the same head over four even counts, and that sustained path can say \"I am listening\". One body part changes only its dynamic, so the audience can see where the idea came from. Jo labels the sharp version \"refusal\" and the sustained version \"patience\".\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two panels contrast equating fast with sharp and noticing that a slow jerk still has a sharp edge.","caption":"Sharp and sustained describe edges and continuity. They are not only another name for fast and slow.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Speed is quality</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Fast always means sharp</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edge and flow</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">A slow jerk can be sharp</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two panels show a sudden stop communicating refusal and an even sustained path communicating waiting.","caption":"Both dynamics are useful. A sharp edge and a sustained flow communicate different ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Sharp dynamic</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Sudden stop can say no</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sustained dynamic</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Even flow can say wait</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-dance-y9-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
